Under Article 39 of the Federal Act on Data Protection, a federal body may process personal data for non-personal purposes such as research, planning or statistics only if these conditions are met:

(a) the data are anonymised as soon as the processing purpose allows;
(b) if the body discloses particularly sensitive personal data to private persons, it does so only in a form that does not allow the data subjects to be identified;
(c) the recipient may pass the data on to third parties only with the consent of the federal body that disclosed them;
(d) the results are published only in a form that does not allow the data subjects to be identified.

Article 39(2) also states that Article 6(3), Article 34(2) and Article 36(1) do not apply to this processing. The passages do not say how a private research institute must be selected or what other terms the agreement with it must contain.
